window.docContents[238].resolve({"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Vector-calculus-and-domain-geometry":{"contents":"Formalized: Euclidean gradient, divergence, and Laplacian; coordinate and\npolar integration formulas; the general coordinate-box Gauss theorem; the\ncirculation (planar Stokes) and flux forms of Green's theorem on rectangles;\nGreen's first and second identities on coordinate boxes and\npositive-radius Euclidean balls; and the sphere's outward normal derivative.\nThe boundary layer includes intrinsic codimension-one Hausdorff measure,\nflat-boundary geometry, and chartwise surface-measure estimates for\noperational  domains. Every operational boundary chart supplies a\nnonvanishing conormal and a chartwise outward unit normal with a proved\norientation law. On chart overlaps the conormals differ by a positive scalar,\nso the normalized vectors define an intrinsic outward unit normal on the\nentire boundary. The inverse charts restricted to the flat hyperplane give\ninjective  parametrizations of the physical boundary pieces, with exact\nimage theorems and continuous, strictly positive tangential norm-determinant\nJacobians. Pushing their Jacobian-weighted parameter measures forward gives\nphysical chart measures, with a Banach-valued change-of-variables formula for\nintegrals. The transition maps between overlapping\nboundary parametrizations are  inverse bijections, their\nJacobian-weighted nonnegative integrals agree, and the resulting physical\nchart measures coincide on overlaps. The  area formula identifies each\nchart measure with codimension-one Hausdorff measure on its chart, so the\nassembled atlas measure is the intrinsic surface measure. On bounded open sets\nwith  graph boundary, the divergence theorem and Green's first and second\nidentities hold for fields that are  (respectively ) up to the\nboundary, with integrability of both sides as part of the conclusion.\n\nPartial: the extension atlas supplies the\nfinite charts, cutoffs, flattening maps, and quantitative estimates required\nby extension, trace, and compactness proofs. Concrete atlases exist for a\nbounded interval and positive-dimensional Euclidean unit balls, and every\nbounded open set with  graph boundary carries one. Theorems on\nLipschitz domains take the atlas as a hypothesis.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Foundational Coverage and Open Problems","header":"3.5.1.3. Vector calculus and domain geometry","id":"/Foundations/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Vector-calculus-and-domain-geometry"},"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Weak-Compactness-for-the-Direct-Method":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Representation Formulas, Variational Principles, and Fixed Points\u0009Minimization and Variations\u0009The Direct Method","header":"9.3.1.4. Weak Compactness for the Direct Method","id":"/Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points/#PDEAtlas--Representation-Formulas___-Variational-Principles___-and-Fixed-Points--Minimization-and-Variations--The-Direct-Method--Weak-Compactness-for-the-Direct-Method"}});